WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.310
Batch size tuning for LLM inference is about matching the model, runtime, hardware, context length, and workload.

2
00:00:07.310 --> 00:00:10.174
It is not a contest to maximize every setting.

3
00:00:10.174 --> 00:00:17.861
A larger value can improve throughput in one situation and damage latency, memory stability, or reliability in another.

4
00:00:17.861 --> 00:00:22.760
The right setting is the one that supports the way the system is actually used.

5
00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:26.754
The most important distinction is between prefill and decode.

6
00:00:26.754 --> 00:00:30.070
Prefill processes the prompt and existing context.

7
00:00:30.070 --> 00:00:31.879
Decode produces new tokens.

8
00:00:31.879 --> 00:00:38.284
Batch settings, prompt length, concurrency, and memory pressure affect those phases differently.

9
00:00:38.284 --> 00:00:46.876
That is why first-token latency, tokens per second, total response time, and requests per second all tell different parts of the story.

10
00:00:46.876 --> 00:00:50.493
Good tuning is measured, conservative, and repeatable.

11
00:00:50.493 --> 00:00:52.603
Start with a realistic workload.

12
00:00:52.603 --> 00:00:54.412
Change one setting at a time.

13
00:00:54.412 --> 00:00:59.311
Track memory, utilization, temperature, errors, latency, and throughput.

14
00:00:59.311 --> 00:01:06.922
Keep enough headroom for normal variation, because a configuration that barely survives a test can fail under real use.

15
00:01:06.922 --> 00:01:11.746
For local interactive systems, prioritize responsiveness and stability.

16
00:01:11.746 --> 00:01:18.604
For API serving, balance user latency with total throughput, fairness, queue behavior, and backpressure.

17
00:01:18.604 --> 00:01:23.879
In both cases, document the settings that work for each model and hardware profile.

18
00:01:23.879 --> 00:01:30.436
The goal is reliable inference that feels predictable, observable, and appropriate for the workload.

19
00:01:30.436 --> 00:01:36.615
The durable lesson is that inference tuning is operational engineering, not a magic number hunt.

20
00:01:36.615 --> 00:01:42.418
The same model can need different settings on a laptop, a workstation, and a shared service.

21
00:01:42.418 --> 00:01:53.798
What carries across environments is the method: define the workload, measure the phases, control memory pressure, and keep the system observable enough to explain its behavior.
